Slide 1 — Introduction

> “Hi everyone, thanks for joining.
This is going to be a very informal session today — more of a quick overview of what the Applied AI team does within Group Control, how we operate and some of the things that may surprise people about working in AI in a bank.

I’ll keep this relatively light and non-technical, so feel free to interrupt with questions as we go rather than waiting until the end.

Most people today interact with AI through things like ChatGPT or Copilot, but our world is slightly different.
Our job is really about using data and AI to help protect the bank — whether that is detecting fraud, identifying suspicious behaviour, improving operational efficiency or helping teams make better decisions at scale.”




---

Slide 2 — Leadership Team

> “So this gives a quick overview of the leadership structure across Applied AI.

What is quite unique about the team is the breadth of problems we work on.
We cover everything from fraud detection through to transaction monitoring, screening and now increasingly agentic AI use cases as well.

The problems themselves are very different, but the underlying challenges are often similar:

large amounts of data,

rapidly evolving behaviours,

and the need to make accurate decisions quickly and safely.


One thing people often underestimate is how multidisciplinary this space is.
It is not just data scientists building models in isolation.

We work very closely with:

product owners,

compliance,

technology,

data teams,

governance,

and monitoring teams.


AI in a large organisation is really a team sport.”




---

Slide 3 — Purpose & Outcomes

> “At a high level, the purpose of the team is fairly simple:

helping Group Control stay resilient and future-ready by using advanced analytics and AI responsibly.

The way I normally simplify this is into three big themes.

First: better protection.
We want to identify more genuinely risky activity and detect emerging patterns earlier.

Second: better efficiency.
A lot of operational teams deal with extremely large alert volumes, so improving precision and reducing false positives can have a massive impact.

And third: trust and governance.
In banking, having an accurate model is not enough.
It also needs to be explainable, monitored, governed and aligned with policy and regulation.

One thing that is really important in this space is balancing risk and efficiency.
The goal is not simply to reduce alerts or automate everything.

The goal is to make better risk decisions while staying within the bank’s risk appetite.

And that is often where the real complexity comes in.”




---

Slide 4 — Operating Model

> “This slide shows a simplified version of how we operate.

One thing people sometimes imagine is that AI teams just build a model, deploy it and move on.

In reality, there is a continuous feedback loop.

It usually starts with stakeholders and product owners identifying a business problem or pain point.

Then the Applied AI team works on:

understanding the data,

building models,

testing approaches,

and validating performance.


But that is only part of the story.

The partnership with technology and data teams is absolutely critical because ultimately these solutions need to run reliably in production, be monitored properly and integrate into operational workflows.

Then monitoring and governance feed back into the process continuously:

checking model stability,

performance drift,

operational effectiveness,

and whether behaviours are changing over time.


In practice, maintaining and evolving models is often harder than building the first version.”




---

Slide 5 — The reality of Applied AI

> “I thought I’d finish with a few things that often surprise people about working in Applied AI.

The first one is probably the biggest:

AI is often the easy part.

Building a model can actually be relatively fast nowadays.
The difficult part is making it production-ready, explainable, monitored and robust enough to operate safely at scale.

The second is that small improvements can create very large impact.

At Barclays scale, even small uplifts in detection rates or reductions in false positives can translate into significant operational and financial benefits.

Another important point is that AI is not really about replacing humans in our space.

Most of the value comes from helping people make better and faster decisions — especially in environments where investigators or analysts are dealing with huge volumes of information.

The field also moves incredibly quickly.

Some techniques that felt very advanced only a few years ago are now becoming standard practice, which means continuous learning and adaptation are really important.

And finally, probably the least glamorous but most important point:

good AI needs strong foundations.

High-quality data, engineering, monitoring and governance are often more important than the actual algorithm itself.”

