Updated Slide 1 — Introduction

> “Hi everyone, thanks for joining.
This is going to be a very informal session today — more of a quick overview of what the Applied AI team does within Group Control, how we operate and some of the things that may surprise people about working in AI in a bank.

I’ll keep this relatively light and non-technical, so feel free to interrupt with questions as we go rather than waiting until the end.

Most people today interact with AI through things like ChatGPT or Copilot, but our world is slightly different.
Our job is really about using data and AI to help protect the bank — whether that is detecting fraud, identifying suspicious behaviour, improving operational efficiency or helping teams make better decisions at scale.

One recent change is that the team is now called ‘Applied AI’ rather than ‘Quantitative Analytics’.
In reality, that is actually a better reflection of what the team has been doing for quite a long time already.

We have always been much more focused on applying analytics and machine learning to real operational problems rather than purely theoretical quantitative work.

For example:

fraud detection models running in production in real time,

transaction monitoring optimisation,

screening models,

model monitoring frameworks,

deployment pipelines,

and increasingly agentic AI use cases.


So the new name is less about a complete shift in direction and more about aligning the name with the reality of the work already happening across the team.”





You will probably get a mix of:

curious non-technical questions

AI hype questions

governance/risk questions

practical operating model questions


The key is to sound relaxed and thoughtful rather than overly polished.

Likely Questions & Natural Answers

“How much of your work is actually GenAI?”

> “Probably less than people would expect today.
Most of the production value currently still comes from more traditional machine learning and analytics.

GenAI is extremely exciting, especially for productivity, investigation support and knowledge retrieval, but predictive ML remains very important for things like fraud detection and transaction monitoring.”




---

“Are you trying to replace humans with AI?”

> “No, not really.
In our space, the biggest value usually comes from helping people make better and faster decisions.

A lot of operational teams deal with huge volumes of alerts and information every day.
AI helps prioritise attention and surface genuinely higher-risk activity rather than removing human judgement entirely.”




---

“What is the biggest challenge in Applied AI?”

> “Honestly, usually not the model itself.

Data quality, deployment, monitoring, governance and operational integration are often much harder than building the first version of a model.

The technical side is only one piece of the puzzle.”




---

“How do you make sure models are trusted?”

> “That is a huge focus area for us.

We spend a lot of time on explainability, governance, monitoring and testing.

In banking, having a powerful model is not enough — people need to understand how it behaves, where it performs well, where it has limitations and how it is monitored over time.”




---

“What does model monitoring actually mean?”

> “It is essentially making sure the model continues to behave as expected once it is in production.

For example:

are the scores shifting unexpectedly,

is data quality changing,

are detection rates stable,

are customer behaviours evolving,

are we seeing drift over time.


Models are not static — the environment around them changes constantly.”




---

“What is agentic AI?”

> “The simplest way to think about it is AI systems that can perform multi-step tasks rather than just answering a single prompt.

So instead of only generating text, an agentic system might:

retrieve information,

analyse it,

make decisions,

trigger workflows,

or interact with multiple tools.


It is still an evolving space, but there is a lot of potential.”




---

“What skills are most important in your team?”

> “There is actually quite a broad mix.

Obviously machine learning and Python are important, but equally:

data engineering,

software engineering,

communication,

problem solving,

and understanding the business context


are all critical.

The best AI solutions usually come from combining technical expertise with strong understanding of operational problems.”




---

“How quickly is the field evolving?”

> “Very quickly.

Some techniques that felt cutting-edge only a few years ago are now becoming fairly standard.

The challenge now is less about whether the technology exists and more about how to operationalise it safely and effectively at scale.”




---

“What is the difference between rules and AI?”

> “Rules are usually very explicit and deterministic: ‘if X happens, trigger Y’.

AI is more about identifying complex patterns and relationships in data that would be difficult to express manually.

In practice, the two work very well together rather than competing with each other.”




---

“Can AI fully prevent fraud or financial crime?”

> “Unfortunately no system is ever perfect because behaviours continuously evolve.

The objective is really about improving detection, adapting faster and helping teams focus attention on the highest-risk activity as effectively as possible.”




---

“What excites you most in the next few years?”

> “Probably the combination of:

stronger AI capabilities,

better platforms,

improved MLOps,

and agentic AI workflows.


I think we are moving toward AI becoming much more embedded into operational decision-making processes rather than existing as isolated models.”




