A few good phrases to reuse naturally

These sound very human/senior in Q&A:

“One thing people often underestimate is…”

“In practice…”

“The reality is…”

“What becomes interesting at Barclays scale is…”

“The difficult part is not building the first version…”

“It is less about replacing and more about augmenting…”

“A model is only one component of the ecosystem…”


These help you sound conversational rather than scripted.




These are actually very good because they are practical and audience-focused rather than overly technical.

I would prepare concise, conversational answers because there is a high chance someone will ask one directly during the session.

“How Applied AI supports data-driven decision making across Group Control”

> “At a high level, we help teams make better decisions using data rather than relying purely on static rules or manual review.

That could mean:

identifying higher-risk activity,

prioritising alerts,

detecting emerging patterns,

or improving operational efficiency.


The important thing is that AI is usually supporting decision-making rather than replacing it entirely.”



You can also add:

> “A lot of the value comes from helping people focus attention where it matters most.”




---

“How data and analytics are used in developing analytical models and machine learning”

> “Everything starts with data.

We use historical behaviours, operational outcomes and domain expertise to identify patterns associated with fraud, suspicious activity or operational risk.

Then we test different modelling approaches to see what provides the best balance between detection performance, explainability and operational usability.

But building the model is only one step — deployment, monitoring and governance are equally important.”



That last sentence is strong because it reinforces your narrative.


---

“Where does Applied AI become relevant to my role?”

This is probably the most important audience question.

> “More than people sometimes realise.

Even if someone is not building models directly, AI increasingly influences:

operational processes,

prioritisation,

decision support,

monitoring,

productivity tools,

and how teams interact with data.


I think over the next few years, understanding how to work alongside AI will become important across almost every function — not just technical roles.”



That sounds forward-looking without sounding scary.


---

“Who to contact / where to go if I need support?”

Keep this informal and open.

> “The easiest starting point is usually reaching out to the Applied AI team or the relevant product owner within GCDA.

A lot of what we do starts from operational pain points or inefficiencies identified by teams themselves.

Even if something is not immediately an AI use case, those conversations are often where the best ideas come from.”



This answer is important because it positions the team as approachable rather than “the AI people in a corner”.


