One extra question you should proactively address

You could even bring this up yourself because somebody will think it.

“Do I need to become technical to work with AI?”

> “Not necessarily.

Technical skills are obviously important for building AI systems, but domain expertise and understanding operational problems are equally valuable.

The best AI solutions usually come from strong collaboration between technical and non-technical teams.”



This lands very well with broader audiences.


---

One thing you can do during Q&A

When answering questions, try using examples like:

fraud alerts

prioritisation

operational efficiency

monitoring

investigator workflows


rather than talking abstractly about “models” and “algorithms”.

People connect much more with:

> “helping investigators focus on the highest-risk alerts”



than:

> “binary classification models with threshold optimisation”.



That will make the session feel much more engaging and accessible.

